## INTRODUCTION
Global energy-related carbon dioxide emissions, which now account for about $65\%$ of all anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions, have risen from approximately $20\mathrm{Gt}$ in 1990 to 31.5 Gt in 2020 contributing to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reaching its highest-ever average annual concentration in the atmosphere of 412.5 ppm.1,2 The capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gases using technologies such as absorption, adsorption, and membranes can mitigate this trend. Aqueous amine absorption (amine scrubbing), while effective,3 requires high energy consumption and must contend with corrosion and degradation issues. The regeneration energy for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption is from 3.24 to $4.2\:\mathrm{GJ}$ /tonne $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{4}$ due to the strong affinity between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and amines and the high heat capacity of the solvent, while the regeneration energy for adsorption lies between 1.9 and $3.23\mathrm{GJ}$ /tonne $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ depending on the adsorbent and cyclic process.5−8 Adsorption also benefits from mild regeneration conditions, desirable adsorption kinetics, absence of corrosive chemicals, and good long-term stability. However, improvements in adsorbent media, contactors, and process designs are necessary to ensure economic feasibility in replacing the existing absorptive technology; viability will rely not only on lowering energy demands but also on reducing the capital cost.
Diverse materials have been investigated for adsorptionbased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture systems, including silicas, carbons, zeolites, metal oxides, and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs).3,4,9−12
MOFs offer high surface areas and pore volumes, tailorability (e.g., diverse metal species, difference in organic linkers, installation of various functional groups), chemical versatility, and good thermal stability. For these reasons, MOFs have been highly studied for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture applications at the laboratory scale; however, the economics of the MOF scale-up remains a largely unanswered question. Functionalizing MOF metal sites with amines has recently been developed as a method to further improve adsorption performance and to engineer the shape of the adsorption isotherm, enabling energy-efficient cyclic adsorption processes.13,14 Choi et al.15 functionalized $\dot{\bf M}{\bf g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})^{\mathrm{~~}}$ (dobdc $=2,5$ -dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) with ethylenediamine (ED) to achieve a stable capacity of approximately 1.51 mmol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -sorb from an ultradilute $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas stream $\left(400\mathrm{\ppm}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ . The expanded framework, $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (dobpdc) ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n},$ $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},$ Fe, Co, Mn; dobpdc $\mathit{\Theta}=4{,}4^{\prime}\mathit{\Theta}=$ dioxidobiphenyl- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate), enables amine functionalization on the open metal sites to further improve $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.16,17 After grafting diamines, adsorption on such
MOFs exhibits a step adsorption isotherm,16−18 which increases the working capacity with smaller temperature swings compared to a classic type I isotherm on open metal site MOFs from 3.3 to $4.1\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -MOF within a temperature swing range of $140{-}35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A specific cyclic $1^{\circ}/2^{\circ}$ diamine, 2- (aminomethyl)piperidine (2-ampd),18 was appended to metal sites in $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ (chemical structure is shown in Figure S1) with good thermal stability and full chemisorption capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low pressures. Through modification of steric interactions of 2-ampd, a unique step adsorption isotherm was observed. Moreover, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was greatly improved under humid conditions. Due to the benefits of such 2-ampd$\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ , this diamine-appended MOF is ideal for incorporating into hollow fibers for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from the natural gas combined cycle (NGCC) through temperature swing adsorption (TSA) processes.
Despite such progress in terms of adsorption media, conventional adsorption contactors still highly rely on packed adsorbent beds with solid particles or expensive monolith contactors. Pellet-based systems suffer from high pressure drops and extended mass transfer zones, resulting in inefficient adsorbent utilization. Moreover, in the special case of 2-ampdappended- $\cdot{\mathrm{M}}_{\mathbf{\ensuremath{g_{2}}}}$ (dobpdc), the nonisothermal operation typically associated with packed beds increases the cost of the separation process.19 Indeed, active thermal management (i.e., cooling during adsorption) has been identified as a critical aspect in the development of sorbents with type V isotherms.19 To develop amine-functionalized MOFs for future implementation and commercialization, an alternative approach is needed. Koros, Lively, and co-workers20−26 previously developed hollow fiber sorbents for rapid cyclic adsorption/desorption processes, which allow for localized thermal management via flowing water in the fiber bore. Hollow fiber sorbents also provide low pressure drop, shorten the mass transfer zone, and enable a rapid temperature swing (i.e., $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption cycle of $<4~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) along with possible effective heat integration if a water-resistant layer is installed on the bore side.
First-generation sorbents used zeolites blended with cellulose acetate (CA) in the fiber wall,20 and later, polyethylenimine (PEI)-infused silica- and amine-grafted silica hollow fiber sorbents were used for improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption capacities under humid conditions.22−25,27 Typically, the preparation of hollow fiber sorbents relies on the use of polar solvents to give uniform sorbent dispersion via sonication, which is ultimately followed by a water quench in the spinning process. These harsh conditions often induce a variety of MOF degradation problems. So far, only MIL$101(\dot{\mathrm{Cr}})^{28}$ and UiO- $\cdot66^{28,29}$ have been reported to survive such a spinning procedure while maintaining nearly $90\%$ of the MOF-based capacities. Formation of monoliths/hollow fibers with several water-sensitive $\mathrm{MOFs}^{30-32}$ was reported using postspinning growth of MOF crystals onto and into precursor monoliths and hollow fibers, respectively. Among this class of fiber sorbents, a $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ hollow fiber sorbent was also reported and prepared through multistep pre-cross-linking of polymer supports and post-treatment of a precursor $\mathrm{(MgO)}$ hollow fiber. As shown in the flow chart of the postspinning process in Figure 1, the complexity of this approach will complicate scale-up, suggesting that more streamlined methods are needed.32
To enable scalable preparation of a $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/2$ -ampd$\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) hollow fibers, the present work aims to develop a simplified protocol for incorporating such MOFs into polymeric fibers for utilization in carbon capture systems. Thus, a direct spinning process is proposed as shown in the flow chart in Figure 1, simplifying the preparation process. Our preliminary work on the stability of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ under real fiber spinning conditions (Figure S2) is the first evidence of directly spinning an open metal site MOF, in contrast to previous studies that have either utilized MOFs without open metal sites or complex postspinning MOF crystal growth methods. In particular, we explored the feasibility of incorporating 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) into conventional commercially available polymers [i.e., cellulose acetate (CA), polyimides (Matrimid and Torlon), and poly(ether sulfone) (PES)] and novel, synthesized polymers [polymer of intrinsic microporosity (PIM-1) and amidoximized PIM-1 (AO-PIM1)]. We also optimized the MOF loading amount and studied the effects of pore formers and spinning conditions. After extrusion and phase separation, the hollow fibers went through methanol exchange followed by diamine postinfusion in methanol. These fibers were used in dynamic breakthrough experiments with simulated NGCC dry flue gas $\left(4.5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ to demonstrate the sorption performance of these materials.
## MATERIALS AND METHODS
## Materials
$\mathrm{Mn}_{0.12}\mathrm{Mg}_{1.88}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ was prepared as published previously.33,34 2- (aminomethyl)piperidine (2-ampd) for diamine grafting was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
Commercially available polymers were used as polymer supports for the MOF and are listed in Table S1. These polymers include cellulose acetate (CA) $\left(M_{\mathrm{w}}\:=\:50,000$ , Sigma-Aldrich), poly(amideimide) (PAI, Torlon, Solvay Advanced Polymers), polyimide Matrimid 5218 (Huntsman International LLC), and poly(ether sulfone) (PES, Veradel 3000P, Solvay). Both lithium nitrate $(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3},$ ReagentPlus) and poly(vinylpyrrolidone) (PVP, $\boldsymbol{M_{\mathrm{w}}}\ =$ 55,000) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich for use as pore formers. In addition, novel polymers, polymer of intrinsic microporosity (PIM-1) and amidoximized PIM-1 (AO-PIM-1), were studied as supports for 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ . The synthesis of PIM-1 followed the low-temperature process described in Jue et al.’s work.35,36 The modification of amidoxime on PIM-1 used the same procedure as our previous work,37 and detailed procedures for synthesizing PIM-1 and AO-PIM-1 are shown in the Supporting Information.
The solvents in the polymer dope solutions are tetrahydrofuran (THF) (anhydrous, with $250~\mathrm{ppm}$ BHT as an inhibitor, ${>}99\%$ , from Sigma-Aldrich) for PIM-1 and $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) (ReagentPlus $99\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich, Milwaukee) for other polymers. The nonsolvent for PIM-1 is $N,N$ -dimethylacetamide (DMAc) (anhydrous, $98.5\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), while deionized water $\left(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}\right)$ was used for other polymers. Methanol $(99.8\%$ , ACS Reagent, SigmaAldrich) and hexanes (ACS Reagent, $98.5\%$ , Baker) were used as solvent exchange agents. Lithium nitrate $\left(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\right)$ ) (ReagentPlus, Sigma-Aldrich) was used as an alternate pore former instead of PVP, as will be explained below.
Some of the chemicals for PIM-1 synthesis were purchased from Fisher Scientific including tetrafluoroterephthalonitrile (TFTPN) $(98\%)$ , $\phantom{-}5,5^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}$ -tetrahydroxy- $^{3,3,3^{\prime},3^{\prime}}.$ ,-tetramethyl- $^{1,1^{\prime}}.$ -spirobisindane (TTSBI) (Alfa Aesar, $97\%$ ), and potassium carbonate $\left(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\right)$ (Alfa Aesar, anhydrous, $99\%$ ). The solvent for the synthesis was $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, anhydrous, $99.8\%$ ), purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Ultrahigh purity (UHP, ${>}99.99\%$ ) gases were purchased from Airgas.
## Fiber Contactor Formation
A range of dope solutions were prepared for syringe extrusion to optimize dope properties in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ vials, and the compositions of each polymer dope are listed in Table S2. Polymer dopes were prepared and mixed homogeneously on a heated roller at about $50{-}60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Afterward, the desired amount of MOF particles was added and blended into the polymer dope. The mixture was sonicated three times for $30~\mathsf{s}$ each with a $1\mathrm{min}$ interval to avoid the collapse of MOF crystals. The sonication horn is a VCX130 model from Sonics & Materials Inc. with a maximum power output of 130 watts and a frequency of $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{kHz}}$ . After extruding by syringe, the fibers were set in a quench bath for complete phase separation, followed by solvent exchange by methanol and hexane. Then, the fibers were dried in a vacuum oven overnight at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
A conventional “dry-jet, wet-quench” spinning method was used for fiber formation. In addition, a third pump with solvent-only solutions was used to remove undesired skin layers. The schematic figure and spinning conditions are shown in Figure S3 and Table S3.
After extrusion, the fibers were placed in deionized water for 3 days for complete solvent removal, with the water being replaced once a day; this process is faster in industrial settings due to roll-to-roll processing capability. Afterward, the fibers underwent a solvent exchange process with methanol three times for $20~\mathrm{min}$ each time. Prior to solvent exchange with hexane, the fibers were immersed in a 5 vol $\%$ 2-ampd/MeOH solution overnight for diamine postinfusion. The fibers were transferred from the methanol bath to a fume hood to air dry for about $^{\textrm{1h}}$ and then dried in a vacuum oven overnight at 50 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## Fiber Sorbent Characterizations
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption performance was tested via a thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA; TA Instruments SDT Q50). The adsorption program and time−temperature profile are shown in Figure S4. For adsorption kinetic analysis (Figure S4a), a pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas flow was introduced into the sample chamber with a 23 sccm flow rate at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after pretreatment with 37 sccm $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $240\ \mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isobar was obtained using 23 sccm $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (using ${\bf N}_{2}$ as a balance gas) with a $1\ {^{\circ}{\bf C}}/{\mathrm{min}}$ ramp rate from 140 to $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at 1 atm, as shown in Figure S4b.
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at different temperatures were measured on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument. Prior to isotherm measurements, the fibers were regenerated under 100 sccm of Ar at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h,}}$ followed by $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at $<10$ μbar at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Lower pressure data points $\left(<6\mathrm{\mmHg}\right)$ were allowed to equilibrate for $\mathsf{1.5h,}$ while higher pressure data points $\left({>}6\mathrm{mmHg}\right)$ ) were considered equilibrated after $<0.01\%$ change over $30~\mathrm{s}$ intervals.
The framework structures before and after treatment in the major spinning conditions were analyzed by $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) using an Empyrean XRD system from PANalytical. The ramp rate was $3^{\circ}/$ min within a range of $2{-}60^{\circ}$ .
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed for MOF morphologies before and after hollow fiber incorporation by a Hitachi SU8230 SEM instrument. For the morphology of hollow fiber sorbents, a Hummer 6 gold/palladium sputter coater was used for sputter-coating with gold to less than $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness before SEM analysis.
A Thermo Scientific iSO50 ATR spectrophotometer was used for attenuated total reflectance-Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) analysis. The spectra were recorded by the KBr technique within a range of $4000{-}650~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ .
$\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra on MOF powders treated with different solvents were obtained from a Bruker Advance IIIHD 500 instrument. Before analysis, MOF powder was suspended in $1{-}2~\mathrm{mL}$ of fresh DMSO- $\cdot d_{6}.$ Afterward, one to two drops of DCl in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added into the suspension, which could be dissolved completely by heating and sonication.
## Permeance Tests
To characterize pore network interconnectivity, pure gas permeances of the hollow fiber sorbent were tested after assembling the module, as shown in Figure S5. The module was kept in a permeation box at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and connected to the feed gas through the shell side at 15, 30, or 100 psig. The flow rate of the permeated gas from the bore side was measured by a bubble flow meter, and the permeance was calculated by eq 1. The results from the average of three modules were used in each case.
$$
\frac{P}{l_{\mathrm{f}}}=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{perm}}}{\pi d_{\mathrm{f}}L_{\mathrm{perm}}(p_{\mathrm{feed}}-p_{\mathrm{perm}})}
$$
Note that $P/l_{\mathrm{f}}$ is the gas permeance, which is the pressure-normalized gas flux; $\scriptstyle Q_{\mathrm{perm}}$ is the flow rate of the permeate; $d_{\mathrm{f}}$ is the fiber outer diameter, while $L_{\mathrm{perm}}$ is the fiber’s effective length; and $p_{\mathrm{feed}}$ and $p_{\mathrm{{perm}}}$ are pressures of the feed gas and permeate gas, respectively. The permeance unit is GPU as described in eq 2.
$$
1\mathrm{GPU}=1\times10^{-6}\frac{\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})}{\mathrm{cm}^{2}\times\mathrm{s}\times\mathrm{cmHg}}
$$
Helium $\left(\mathrm{He}\right)$ , nitrogen $\left(\Nu_{2}\right)$ , oxygen $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ , and carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ permeances were tested under $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 100 psig.
## Breakthrough Tests
A $38.74~\mathrm{cm}$ long stainless-steel tube ( $0.95~\mathrm{cm}$ OD with $0.09\ \mathrm{cm}$ wall thickness) was packed with $\ensuremath{4.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ of fibers (24 fibers with approx. 68 wt $\%$ 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) in total). The fiber module was wrapped in heat tape and activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 139 sccm argon flow for 18 h before the first breakthrough and regenerated under the same conditions for 4 h between subsequent breakthroughs. The desorption times were extended significantly past what was required as an extra precaution. Figure S6 shows a near-zero $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration after approximately $15~\mathrm{min}$ under these desorption conditions. At the end of regeneration, the mass spectrometer (MS) inlet was opened, and the outlet gas was scanned to ensure full regeneration (no $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ signal). The heat was adjusted to the analysis temperature (40 or $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}^{\cdot}$ ) and the module was allowed to cool under argon flow. The flue gas mixture containing $4.5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $4.5\%$ He balanced with $\Nu_{2}$ was directed to the MS in a bypass line and scanned to ensure the lines were clean (only $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ He, $\mathrm{N}_{2.}^{\cdot}$ ). The flue gas was then directed through the bed with the following $\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{z}$ ratios measured: 4 $\left(\mathrm{He}\right)$ , 14 $\left(\Nu_{2}\right)$ , 18 $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ , 20 $(\mathrm{Ar})$ , 40 $(\mathrm{Ar})$ , and 44 $\left(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\right)$ .
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and He concentrations as a function of time were used to find the adsorbent capacity. Helium was used as a tracer to account for the dead volume in the system. The $C/C_{0}$ value for each was determined by dividing $C(t)$ by $C_{0}$ (feed concentration). Equation 3 was then used to find the adsorption capacity,
$$
q_{\mathrm{CO}_{2}}=\frac{Q\cdot C_{0,\mathrm{CO}_{2}}}{m_{\mathrm{s}}}\int_{0}^{t_{\mathrm{s}}}\left(\frac{C_{\mathrm{He}}(t)}{C_{0,\mathrm{He}}}-\frac{C_{\mathrm{CO}_{2}}(t)}{C_{0,\mathrm{CO}_{2}}}\right)[s]\mathrm{d}t
$$
where $q_{\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ is the adsorption capacity $[\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{g}];$ Q is the total gas flow rate $[\mathrm{mol}/s]$ , $C_{0,\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ is the feed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration [mol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}.$ /total mol], $C_{0,\mathrm{He}}$ is the feed helium concentration [mol He/total mol], and $m_{\mathrm{s}}$ is the mass of the sorbent in the bed $[\mathbf{g}]$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Mg2(dobpdc) Hollow Fiber Sorbent Spinning
In our preliminary work on polymer selection, the MOF was preserved regardless of which polymer support was used (shown in Supporting Information, Figure S7). $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption kinetic performance shown in Figure S8 on different polymer-supported 2-ampd- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) samples were different due to their intrinsic diffusion coefficient, skin layer formation, and pore structure after phase separation. The final $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of the fiber (Figure S9) was not only contributed by the MOF but also by the polymer networks, as some of the polymers are capable of adsorbing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Among the tested polymer networks, PES was found to have the best performance as it exhibits good thermal stability (Figure S10) and fast adsorption kinetics due to the open pore structure, as shown in Figure S11. CA and AO-PIM-1 can also perform as polymer supports; however, the pore structures of both polymers are denser than PES, leading to transport resistance, as shown in Figure S11. Moreover, the MOF/CA hollow fiber forms a skin layer, which requires a third solvent pump during spinning to prevent the formation of this skin (Figure S12 and Table S3). PIM-1 was difficult to blend with the MOF homogeneously as a result of the usage of a highly volatile solvent (i.e., tetrahydrofuran, one of the few “spinnable” solvents for PIM-1) in the dope solution. Polyimide-based polymers, Matrimid and Torlon, on the contrary, were unable to support 2-ampd- $\cdot\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), which was tentatively attributed to the degradation of imides via amine exposure. Thus, PES-supported 2-ampd- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) hollow fibers were directly spun through a conventional “dry-jet, wet-quench” spinning process, as shown in Figure S3.
To spin MOF/PES, a PES dope with $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ was prepared for blending with 20 and 60 wt $\%$ $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc). Detailed dope compositions and spinning conditions are listed in Table S4. The cross section of the MOF hollow fiber can be seen in Figure 2a, revealing MOF crystals residing in the PES matrix, and a defective skin layer is also shown in Figure 2. As observed in the SEM images, no obvious skin layer was formed, in contrast to the CA-based hollow fibers shown in Figure S12. Pure gas permeances of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{(dobpdc)/PES}$ hollow fibers are shown in Table 1. These permeances are much higher than those of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)/\mathrm{C}\bar{\mathrm{A}}$ hollow fibers (Table S8, Supporting Information). $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeance at 30 psi of 20 wt $\%$ $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PES hollow fibers was 168,000 GPU, which was approximately 10 times higher than that of 20 wt $\%$ $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpd\bar{c}}\big)/\mathrm{CA}$ hollow fibers, promoting much faster $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Such high gas permeances can be attributed to the open pore structure (as shown in Figure 2b) as well as percolating defects through the outer layer of the PES matrix (Figure 2d), which indicates that the polymer network barely hinders the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on MOF in hollow fibers. When the MOF loading increased to $60\ \mathrm{wt}\ \%$ , gas permeance was further improved to 161,400 GPU at 15 psi. Additionally, compared with the gas permeance in other works on hollow fiber sorbents, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ permeance through MOF/PES is much higher than that through other hollow fiber sorbents, as listed in Table S8.
## Post-Spinning Infusion of 2-ampd on Mg (dobpdc)/PES Hollow Fiber Sorbents
Initial attempts at fiber spinning used MOFs that had already been appended with 2-ampd. While the framework of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{do}\bar{\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\big)$ survived spinning conditions (Figure S2), we observed that the diamines were partially stripped away due to the strong affinity between amines and polar solvents (i.e., water in phase separation and methanol in solvent exchange). The loss is shown by a loss of the step in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isobar (Figure S13) and a loss of proton signals in $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR tests (Figure S14). Due to the steric hindrance of 2-ampd in the framework, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isobar on 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdotM}_{\mathbf{\cdot}}$ (dobpdc) has a rare two-step adsorption profile upon decreasing the adsorption temperature.18 While $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/polymer composite contactors retain some $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities (on open metal sites through type I adsorption, as in Table S5), the bare MOF tends to degrade gradually in air or flue gas when it associates with preadsorbed polar solvent molecules.38,39 Thus, to preserve the stability of the MOF and enable the desired step $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior, a procedure for appending diamines to the MOF in polymer contactors was necessary.
Considering this, we developed a scalable method for appending diamines on the MOF within a polymer network for hollow fiber sorbents.40 In a typical fiber formation procedure, the hollow fibers are soaked in MeOH and hexane after completing phase separation in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The solvent exchange process minimizes pore collapse. However, the standard procedure for grafting 2-ampd onto $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ uses toluene sasp anncianrgr epr oscolevsesndt,u1e8 twohiitcshsiwseallninugnedffesicrteodnsomlvoestntpdoluyrimnegrst.h4e1 However, MeOH is a good solvent and carrier for amine chemicals into porous materials via capillary action. Labreche aGas permeances were tested at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
et al.22,25 introduced polyethylenimine (PEI) into porous materials/polymer hollow fiber sorbents by dissolving it in MeOH, and the resultant sorbents showed improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance. Inspired by that work, we used MeOH instead of toluene as an infusion medium to carry 2- ampd through the porous polymer, and the infused-diamines were grafted to the open metal sites in the MOF. Figure 3a compares $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isobars of MOF/PES fiber sorbents for which MeOH and toluene were used as solvents for infusion. The two-step adsorption is evident for both 2-ampd/toluene and 2- ampd/MeOH infusion procedures, and both are consistent with that of pure 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{g}_{2}}$ (dobpdc).18 Moreover, the fiber sorbent infused in 2-ampd/MeOH shows steeper, more discernible adsorption steps than that in 2-ampd/toluene, which can be ascribed to toluene-induced deformation of the PES pore structure. Furthermore, the final uptake at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on the fiber sorbent is about $11.62~\mathrm{\g/100}$ which is approximately $73.5\%$ of that on the pure MOF (i.e., 15.81 $\bar{\bf g/100\textsubscript{g})}$ . This result is consistent with the MOF loading in a mixed matrix dope before spinning (i.e., 75 wt $\%$ MOF in total fiber), indicating that $98\%$ of the MOFs in the fiber are utilized for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. The difference in the final uptake of the MOF at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ between this work and Siegelman et al.’s work18 is ascribed to the different batches of MOFs from different sources. Thus, if a batch of MOF with higher sorption capacity is prepared, the fiber will achieve a higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake. Of note, the initial adsorption step temperature of $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is slightly lower than the step temperature of pure MOF samples. Such a shift in temperature can be associated with the thermal moderating effect caused by polymer networks. Another explanation may be due to mass transfer resistance ascribed to the porosity and the formed skin layer. In addition, residual solvent in the MOF may also contribute to the temperature shift.
Since the porous structure of the polymer network can also support diamine s,40 whether the diamines intermix with the polymer network or successfully append on metal sites is of great interest and deserves further investigation. For this purpose, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isobars on the pure PES fiber and MOF/PES fiber, prepared and infused in 2-ampd/MeOH, are compared in Figure S15. The pure PES infused with diamines were found to be incapable of adsorbing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at any appreciable level, demonstrating that the high levels of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake in the amineappended MOF/PES fibers are a result of amine−MOF interactions. In addition to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance, we also observed translucent substance on the surface of 2- ampd/PES fibers after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ exposure (Figure $\mathrm{{s15b,c}}$ ), indicating that 2-ampd was physically packed in the pores of PES fibers. We also utilized a cyclic temperature swing test to provide evidence that the diamines are appended to metal sites within the MOFs. If diamines intermix with the polymer network, the diamines could be easily swept away through adsorption/desorption cycles due to the weak interaction between diamines and the polymer, ultimately leading to a drop in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake. Figure 3b shows the overlapping results of 10 adsorption/desorption cycles of the cyclic temperature swing test from 110 to $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The two-step adsorption occurs within all tested cycles, demonstrating the stable appending of diamine on the MOF inside the polymer network, as the amine polymer by itself is unable to afford a stepped isotherm or isobar. Furthermore, as shown in Figure 3c, the cyclic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake changes little, stabilizing at 2.2 mmol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -fiber and 2.7 mmol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}–\mathrm{MOF}$ . The value slightly lower than the theoretical value (i.e., $2.8~\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -fiber) presumably reflects the inadequate adsorption time of the isobar adsorption technique; the value at equilibrium coincides with the 75 wt $\%$ uptake, as shown in Figure S16. Additionally, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake can also be improved by optimizing the infusion conditions, which will be discussed in our future work.
To verify the versatility of this postinfusion approach, various amines were infused onto $\bar{\bf M}{\bf g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PES. As shown in Figure S17, the isobars of these as-prepared adsorbents are analogous to those of the corresponding pure amine-appended MOFs. Therefore, the developed postinfusion approach is applicable to various diamine or triamine/MOF combinations, not only endowing them with chemical versatility but also offering alternatives to engineering these materials based on application-driven purposes.
## ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ Adsorption Performance on 2-ampd-Mg2(dobpdc)/PES Hollow Fiber Sorbents
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm on 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PES hollow fiber sorbents at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is shown in Figure 4a. The isotherm at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibits the characteristic two-step adsorption due to the steric hindrance by 2-ampd on $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\bar{\mathrm{dobpdc}}\big)$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at 40 mbar was approximately $2.0~\mathrm{{\mmol}}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -fiber or $3.0~\mathrm{~mmol}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{g}$ -MOF. In Siegelman et al.’s work,18 the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake on pure MOF powder at 40 mbar and $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was about $3.4\mathrm{\mmol}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/{\bf g}_{-}$ sorb. Determined by the weight loss via TGA (Figure S18), the MOF loading is about 68 wt $\%$ in the total fiber. This shows that nearly $88\%$ of the MOF initially in the dope was maintained after spinning into hollow fibers, indicating the scalable application of 2-ampd- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) hollow fiber sorbents.
The ultimate goal of this work is to utilize the hollow fiber sorbents in industrial applications. Therefore, breakthrough tests on 2-ampd- $\cdot\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PES hollow fibers for a simplified $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture demonstration were conducted. 2- ampd- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/PES hollow fibers were aligned in a fixedbed module with a void fraction of 0.35 (24 fibers). The cross section of the module and the fibers’ flexibility are shown in Figure $^{4\mathrm{b,d}}$ . A simulated NGCC exhaust gas containing 4.5 $\mathrm{mol}\ \%\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ with a He tracer was used as the feed gas. The experimental details are described in the Experimental Section. The breakthrough curves at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Figure 4c, in which a similar “shock-wave-shock” breakthrough to the MOF particle fixed bed18 was observed. Such a breakthrough trend is attributed to the step adsorption of the 2-ampd- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}^{\mathbf{\bar{\alpha}}}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ in hollow fibers. The second step occurs at a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\ C/C_{0}$ of approximately 0.1, which was at $0.4\mathrm{vol}\mathrm{\}96\mathrm{\CO}_{2}$ concentration. As seen in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherm at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Figure 4a, the second step occurs at about 4 mbar, which is consistent with the breakthrough curve. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was $2.5\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ${\bf C O}_{2}/{\bf g}–{\bf M}{\bf O}{\bf F}$ , lower than the isotherm uptake at 40 mbar (i.e., 3.0 mmol ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{g}}–{\mathrm{MOF}},$ ), which matches the breakthrough results in Siegelman et al.’s work on the MOF particles in a fixed bed.18 Compared to the fixed-bed particle sorbents, both “steps” of the breakthrough curve are sharper in the hollow fiber sorbent bed, which is indicative of more rapid mass transfer, as expected. In addition, the hollow fiber morphology has a benefit of a lower pressure drop compared to a conventional particle/pellet sorption bed, which was reported in our previous work.19,42
## CONCLUSIONS
The high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and water stability of 2-ampd$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ make it an appealing candidate for incorporation into hollow fiber composite contactors. The protocols we report for creating 2-ampd- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc)/polymer hollow fiber sorbents via a direct spinning process are straightforward and scalable, eliminating complex steps in postspinning processes. Three polymers, CA, PES, and AO-PIM-1, are workable as polymer networks, while polyimides, such as Matrimid and Torlon, degrade in contact during the diamine infusion. Despite the lack of explicit study of kinetics, the results of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm and dynamic breakthrough experiments were consistent with those on MOF powder beds but with qualitatively faster adsorption kinetics and a lower pressure drop.
Nonetheless, there are some key limitations in our current work. First, this work is limited to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture under dry conditions. As the humidity in postcombustion gas is nonnegligible, the influence of humidity on MOF hollow fiber sorbents is important and will be studied in our future work (although it is likely that the moisture will promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, as it does on pure 2-ampd- $\mathrm{.Mg_{2}(d o b p d c)^{18})}$ . Moreover, for a better utilization of MOF hollow fiber sorbents with heat integration through an RTSA process, a scalable one-step formation of a water-proof lumen layer in the bore side will be studied and investigated in subsequent work. Additionally, while energy analysis is beyond the scope of this work, the regeneration energy of an MOF hollow fiber is expected to be approximately $73~\mathrm{kJ/mol},^{18}$ similar to a pure MOF due to the high loading of the MOF in the fiber.43 With the study of heat integration and energy analysis, breakthrough modeling will be reported in future work focusing on utilizing MOF hollow fibers in a TSA process with thermal management, which has been studied in our previous work on hollow fiber sorbents using a TSA process.44,45 In spite of these limitations, the simplified process described in this work is the first step toward utilizing novel and high-performance MOFs in industrial carbon capture processes.
